      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 534,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the Earth’s surface. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The \"Magnet\" Effect (Polarity):** Water molecules are polar. The oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. Because of this, water molecules attract each other like tiny magnets (forming \"hydrogen bonds\"). This unique bonding gives water its remarkable properties, such as high surface tension and the ability to dissolve many substances.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states:\n* **Liquid:** The most common form, found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). *Uniquely, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.*\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Role on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop—evaporating from the surface, condensing into clouds, falling as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back into oceans and rivers.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is often called the \"solvent of life.\" \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. Cells consist mostly of water, and it is the medium in which all the metabolic and chemical reactions of life take place.\n* **Survival:** While a human can survive for weeks without food, they can generally only survive for about 3 to 5 days without water.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
